VI  - 336
DP  - 2026 May 21
TI  - Generative AI in Dutch General Practice: A Pilot Study of Current Use and Future 
      Perspectives.
PG  - 899-903
LID - 10.3233/SHTI260309 [doi]
AB  - Dutch general practice is under increasing pressure from workforce shortages and 
      administrative workload. Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is being 
      promoted as a potential way to support documentation, communication, and clinical 
      reasoning, yet real-world use and evaluation in primary care remain 
      insufficiently understood. This exploratory pilot study examined how Dutch 
      general practitioners (GPs) currently use GenAI, what benefits and barriers they 
      perceive, and which conditions they consider necessary for responsible 
      implementation. Thirteen GPs participated in semi-structured interviews; data 
      collection continued until thematic saturation was judged to be reached. 
      Reflexive thematic analysis identified five themes: (1) support without replacing 
      clinical judgment; (2) the importance of integration into the electronic health 
      record (EHR) and workflows; (3) privacy, governance, and liability as major 
      barriers; (4) tensions with professional identity and role boundaries; and (5) 
      trust linked to transparency and interpretive literacy. As an additional 
      descriptive layer, manual sentiment scoring of 140 coded excerpts was performed. 
      Overall, participants viewed GenAI as potentially valuable when well-integrated 
      and appropriately governed but expressed clear conditions for safe and acceptable 
      use. These findings are context-specific and hypothesis-generating and should not 
      be interpreted as generalizable beyond this exploratory sample.
