TI  - The mediating role of critical thinking between AI literacy and learning burnout 
      in nursing students using generative AI: A cross-sectional descriptive study.
PG  - 107169
LID - S0260-6917(26)00197-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.nedt.2026.107169 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Learning burnout hinders nursing students' academic and professional 
      development. Despite the growing integration of artificial intelligence (AI) in 
      education, the mediating role of critical thinking between AI literacy and 
      learning burnout remains unclear in nursing education. AIMS: This study aims to 
      explore the relationships among AI literacy, learning burnout and critical 
      thinking, and to investigate whether critical thinking mediates the relationship 
      between AI literacy and learning burnout in nursing undergraduates using 
      generative AI tools. DESIGN: A cross-sectional descriptive study. METHODS: A 
      convenience sample of 617 undergraduate nursing students was recruited from seven 
      universities across China during July 2025. Data were collected using a 
      demographic and AI usage questionnaire, the Learning Burnout of Undergraduates 
      Scale, the Critical Thinking Scale and the Artificial Intelligence Literacy 
      Scale. Descriptive statistics summarized the demographic characteristics, 
      correlation analyses examined variable relationships, and PROCESS macro (Model 4) 
      tested critical thinking's mediation role. RESULTS: The average scores for 
      learning burnout, AI literacy, and critical thinking were 55.86 (SD 10.90), 70.17 
      (SD 7.59) and 87.68 (SD 9.42). Critical thinking was negatively correlated with 
      learning burnout (r = -0.54, P < 0.001) and positively correlated with AI 
      literacy (r = 0.44, P < 0.001). AI literacy showed a weak negative correlation 
      with learning burnout (r = -0.18, P < 0.01). Mediation analysis revealed that 
      critical thinking fully mediated the relationship between AI literacy and 
      learning burnout (total effect = -0.261, 95%CI [-0.373, -0.150], indirect 
      effect = -0.364, 95% CI [-0.444, -0.293]; direct effect = 0.103, 95% CI [-0.003, 
      0.210]). CONCLUSION: Nursing undergraduates reporting moderate learning burnout. 
      Critical thinking fully mediated AI literacy's alleviating effect on burnout. 
      Therefore, integrating AI literacy with critical thinking is essential for 
      nursing students' well-being in AI-enhanced learning.
