TI  - Artificial Intelligence and Communication Sciences and Disorders: A Bidirectional 
      Frontier.
PG  - 1-17
LID - 10.1159/000552551 [doi]
AB  - This paper explores the bidirectional interplay between Artificial Intelligence 
      (AI) and Communication Sciences and Disorders (CSD). It begins by defining 
      essential terminology and foundational concepts, followed by a discussion of key 
      ethical considerations in the development and deployment of AI technologies. 
      Current AI research in CSD is then reviewed, with particular attention to 
      multimodal approaches that integrate linguistic and nonverbal behavioral cues, 
      such as motion. The paper further illustrates how insights from CSD-and the 
      clinical expertise of Speech-Language Pathologists-can inform the design of AI 
      systems, making them more inclusive, interpretable, and responsive to the 
      diversity of communication disorders. Extending this discussion, we introduce the 
      concept of a CSD Digital Twin: a computational framework capable of analyzing and 
      simulating communication processes in real time to support precision diagnostics, 
      personalized intervention, scientific discovery, and public health planning. 
      Finally, we highlight the importance of cultivating AI literacy-particularly 
      statistical thinking-as a core professional competency for both researchers and 
      clinicians in the AI era. Ultimately, this work envisions a shared framework for 
      advancing research and clinical practice, fostering a data-driven foundation for 
      innovation, discovery, and evidence-based progress in the field of Communication 
      Sciences and Disorders.
