TI  - Nurses' experiences regarding role boundaries in collaborative AI-Assisted 
      nursing: A meta-synthesis.
PG  - 119385
LID - S0277-9536(26)00461-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.socscimed.2026.119385 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly integrating into the nursing 
      field, evolving from simple assistive tools to complex applications that 
      transform nursing practice models, thereby profoundly impacting the professional 
      jurisdiction of nurses. Given the lack of systematic theoretical integration in 
      existing research, this study employs Abbott's Jurisdictional Framework and uses 
      meta-synthesis to reveal how nurses construct and maintain their professional 
      jurisdiction in AI-assisted nursing, as well as the institutional conditions that 
      facilitate or constrain this process. METHODS: A systematic search of six major 
      databases, including PubMed and Embase, identified 12 high-quality qualitative 
      studies from the Netherlands, Turkey, Saudi Arabia, Egypt, Jordan, China, 
      Bangladesh, Australia, the United Kingdom, and the United States. Data analysis 
      followed Thomas and Harden's three-phase thematic synthesis: Phases 1 and 2 
      involved inductive coding to generate 22 descriptive themes; Phase 3 incorporated 
      Abbott's Jurisdictional Framework to synthesize and elevate these into 7 
      analytical themes. RESULTS: Nurses do not passively accept technology in 
      human-machine collaboration but actively engage in "boundary work." They ensure 
      their leading role in clinical reasoning by exercising "final verification 
      authority" and "decision deviation authority," while upholding their right to 
      make personalized treatment adjustments. Facing technological disruption, nurses 
      establish a professional core barrier distinct from machines by emphasizing the 
      irreplaceability of "humanistic care." However, this adaptation process faces 
      significant obstacles in legal liability determination, skills training, and 
      social trust building, hindering effective nurse-AI collaboration. CONCLUSION: 
      The jurisdictional integration between AI and nursing remains in a transitional 
      state of "not yet achieved." At cognitive and discursive levels, nurses are 
      active boundary-makers; at institutional and organizational levels, they are 
      responders to structural constraints. Achieving a sustainable human-machine 
      collaboration ecosystem requires systematic support through legal liability 
      clarification, algorithm literacy education, and organizational resource 
      optimization.
