TI  - Visual memory in Parkinson's Disease: a non-graphomotor approach to assessment.
LID - 10.1007/s00702-026-03181-5 [doi]
AB  - Parkinson's disease (PD) is a progressive motor disorder with multiple motor and 
      non-motor symptoms. Cognitive decline, particularly memory deficits are well 
      documented phenomenon due to its importance as potential biomarkers. While 
      substantial research has explained mechanisms and findings related to deficits in 
      verbal learning and memory, deficits across visual learning and memory are 
      comparatively under-documented. They are often inconsistent due to the reliance 
      on traditional paper-pencil based tests which are susceptible to confounding by 
      motor symptoms and graphomotor deterioration seen in PD. This study aims to 
      explore the usefulness of the Wechsler Memory Scale-IV (WMS-IV) as a 
      non-graphomotor method to testing visual memory profiles in PD. Performance on 
      subtests of the WMS-IV was compared between 113 patients with PD and 90 
      education-matched healthy controls (HC) with stratification based on literacy 
      into literate, functional illiterate and illiterate. Significant differences were 
      observed on sub-tests of Symbol Span and Designs I(Content) scores between the PD 
      groups and their corresponding HC irrespective of education level. Only the 
      literate PD group showed significant differences from their HC on spatial 
      addition subtests. On the Designs I and II spatial scores, functional 
      illiterates, did not differ from their corresponding HC while the literate and 
      illiterate Groups continued to differ. These findings highlight the need to adapt 
      traditional visual learning and memory tests to PD in order to elicit truly 
      representative deficits, and the need to develop alternative versions that are 
      not driven by education with localised education-based norms. These will permit 
      accurate and equitable profiling across literacy backgrounds.
