DP  - 2026 May 13
TI  - Nursing Educators' Utility, Preparedness, and Attitudes Toward Generative 
      Artificial Intelligence in Teaching and Assessment: A Scoping Review.
LID - 10.1097/NNE.0000000000002181 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: This scoping review examined literature on nursing educators' 
      attitudes, preparedness, and utility of generative artificial intelligence 
      (GenAI) in teaching and assessment, noting that how educators are responding 
      within curricula, assessment strategies, and professional development remains 
      unclear. PROBLEM: Limited evidence exists on nursing educators' perceptions of 
      GenAI and readiness to integrate it ethically and pedagogically in academia. 
      METHODS: Following Joanna Briggs Institute guidelines, the review included 
      empirical, peer‑reviewed studies on GenAI utility, attitudes, and preparedness 
      among tertiary nurse educators. Search across Scopus, Web of Science, and 
      Cumulative Index to Nursing and Allied Health Ultimate. Literature excluded 
      opinion pieces and conference proceedings. RESULTS: Fifty‑four studies revealed 4 
      themes: GenAI utility, educational preparation, attitudes, and future 
      recommendations. Findings highlight GenAI's potential to streamline academic 
      tasks, need for digital literacy training, ethical guidance, and 
      discipline‑specific strategies. CONCLUSIONS: GenAI offers transformative 
      potential for simulation, assessment, and academic support. Successful adoption 
      requires institutional support, ethical awareness, and understanding of long-term 
      impacts on learning.
