PMID- 42117781
OWN - NLM
STAT- Publisher
LR  - 20260517
IS  - 1935-7877 (Print)
IS  - 1935-7877 (Linking)
DP  - 2026 May 12
TI  - Survey of student perceptions and use of artificial intelligence when reading 
      primary literature in the biological sciences.
PG  - e0035525
LID - 10.1128/jmbe.00355-25 [doi]
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) tools are increasingly used by students in higher 
      education, yet little is known about how they are applied when reading primary 
      scientific literature. This study investigated how undergraduate biology students 
      use AI while engaging with primary research articles and how such use influences 
      confidence, engagement, and perceived learning. Using a mixed-methods design 
      across two institutions, we collected pre-course surveys assessing prior AI 
      experience and attitudes, weekly self-reported AI usage logs during a 
      semester-long primary literature-based course, and post-course surveys evaluating 
      changes in practices and perceptions. Students entered the course with broad 
      familiarity with generative AI but expressed uncertainty and concern regarding 
      accuracy, ethics, and overreliance. Throughout the semester, students primarily 
      used AI to clarify terminology, methods, figures, and background concepts, with 
      usage patterns evolving toward more targeted and sophisticated prompting. 
      Students also reported increasing awareness of AI limitations and the need for 
      critical evaluation of AI-generated responses. Post-course results indicated 
      increased confidence in analyzing primary data and deeper engagement with the 
      literature, while concerns about overreliance largely diminished. Overall, 
      students viewed AI as a supportive tool that complemented, rather than replaced, 
      their own reasoning. These findings suggest that AI can enhance students' 
      engagement with primary literature when used thoughtfully and underscore the 
      importance of explicit instructional guidance to promote ethical, effective, and 
      critical use of AI in biological science education.
