DP  - 2026 May 12
TI  - Artificial Intelligence Literacy and Utilization Barriers in Nursing Learning: A 
      Qualitative Exploration.
PG  - 1-9
LID - 10.3928/01484834-20260406-02 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The artificial intelligence (AI) transformation of nursing education 
      hinges on students' AI literacy. This study explores nursing students' AI 
      literacy and utilization barriers in AI-assisted learning. METHOD: A descriptive 
      qualitative design was used. Twenty-six undergraduate nursing students were 
      recruited. Data were collected between May 2024 and January 2025 through 
      individual, in-depth, semistructured interviews. Data analysis followed Clarke 
      and Braun's thematic analysis approach. RESULTS: Five main themes were 
      identified: (1) ambiguous perceptions of AI; (2) adaptive behaviors toward AI 
      technology; (3) ambivalent attitudes toward AI applications; (4) perceived 
      barriers to AI adoption; and (5) expectations of AI integration. CONCLUSION: This 
      qualitative study illuminates the multifaceted landscape of AI literacy and 
      barriers perceived by nursing students, and underscores the need for nursing 
      education to address the AI literacy gaps and overcome adoption barriers. Future 
      research should track the long-term effects of AI literacy on clinical 
      performance and career trajectories.
