PMC - PMC13142078
OAB - This study asked occupational therapists at a big Australian hospital what they 
      think about using artificial intelligence (AI) in their job. We asked 53 
      therapists questions about their AI knowledge and what training they want. Most 
      therapists have almost no experience with AI. They say their knowledge, skills, 
      and confidence are low, but they are excited about AI. Therapists who knew more 
      about AI also felt more confident using it, but where they worked or their years 
      of experience did not make much difference. They think AI could help patients by 
      making assessments easier and reducing paperwork. They are worried about ethical 
      problems and feel unprepared because they have had little training. Therapists 
      want clear, practical training and simple workplace rules. This will help them 
      use AI safely and well, so they can keep helping people live better, more 
      meaningful lives.
