TI  - Ethical dimensions of artificial intelligence integration into dental practice.
LID - S0002-8177(26)00115-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.adaj.2026.02.004 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly applied in dentistry to 
      enhance diagnosis and treatment planning. Its integration raises ethical 
      challenges concerning privacy, consent, algorithmic fairness, and professional 
      accountability. TYPES OF STUDIES REVIEWED: The authors synthesized peer-reviewed 
      literature from dental and medical databases. Data were extracted from studies in 
      which researchers addressed ethical principles, legal implications, and 
      regulatory perspectives on AI use in dentistry. RESULTS: Four major ethical 
      domains of autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, and justice were included as 
      central to AI in dentistry. Key concerns include potential bias in algorithms, 
      inequitable technology access, and threats to data confidentiality. The 
      literature supports developing regulatory frameworks and ethical guidelines to 
      ensure safe, fair, and accountable AI implementation in dental settings. 
      CONCLUSIONS AND PRACTICAL IMPLICATIONS: Responsible AI integration demands 
      transparency, patient involvement, and ethical literacy among dental care 
      professionals. Establishing governance standards and interdisciplinary dialogue 
      will help preserve trust and fairness in AI-assisted oral health care.
