VI  - 163
DP  - 2026 Aug
TI  - Artificial intelligence literacy, readiness, and innovativeness: Exploring 
      interrelationships in undergraduate nursing students.
PG  - 107117
LID - S0260-6917(26)00145-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.nedt.2026.107117 [doi]
AB  - AIM/OBJECTIVE: This study aimed to examine the relationships between AI literacy, 
      medical AI readiness, and individual innovativeness among nursing students. 
      BACKGROUND: Integrating artificial intelligence (AI) into healthcare requires 
      future nurses to develop competencies in literacy, readiness, and innovativeness. 
      Evidence on how these domains interrelate in nursing education remains limited. 
      DESIGN: A cross-sectional, relational study design was adopted. METHODS: The 
      study was conducted between April and July 2025 with 440 nursing students from 
      two state universities in Türkiye. Data were collected online using a Descriptive 
      Information Form, the AI Literacy Scale, the Medical AI Readiness Scale, and the 
      Individual Innovativeness Scale. Descriptive statistics, independent samples 
      t-test, one-way ANOVA, Mann-Whitney U test, Kruskal-Wallis test, Pearson 
      correlation, and multiple regression analysis were used. RESULTS: Students' mean 
      age was 21.13 (SD = 2.80), and 70.5% were female. Most participants (91.4%) had 
      not received prior AI training. University-based comparisons showed significant 
      differences in AI literacy (p = .042), medical AI readiness skills (p = .006), 
      and innovativeness (p = .001). Demographic variables such as gender, grade level, 
      AI training, usage frequency, and openness to new technologies were associated 
      with scale scores. Correlation analyses revealed moderate positive associations 
      between AI literacy and both medical AI readiness (r = 0.532, p < .01) and 
      innovativeness (r = 0.423, p < .01). Regression analyses indicated that AI 
      literacy (β = 0.475, p < .001) and individual innovativeness (β = 0.134, 
      p = .003) significantly predicted AI readiness, while university was not a 
      significant predictor, and the model explained 29.8% of the variance. 
      CONCLUSIONS: AI literacy, readiness, and innovativeness are interrelated 
      competencies among nursing students. Findings provide evidence for integrating 
      AI-oriented content into curricula and for developing strategies that enhance 
      students' preparedness for technology-driven healthcare.
