TI  - Evaluating the performance of large language models versus prosthodontic 
      residents on the 2024 and 2025 National Prosthodontic Resident Examination.
LID - S0022-3913(26)00234-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.prosdent.2026.03.043 [doi]
AB  - STATEMENT OF PROBLEM: Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) offer 
      expanded reasoning, multimodal processing, and improved factual grounding. 
      However, their performance relative to prosthodontic residents remains unknown, 
      and comparisons can inform whether current training fosters higher-order 
      reasoning beyond the capabilities of artificial intelligence (AI). PURPOSE: This 
      study compared the performance of 3 advanced LLMs, ChatGPT-5, Gemini 2.5 Pro, and 
      Claude Sonnet 4, with prosthodontic residents on the 2024 and 2025 National 
      Prosthodontic Resident Examinations (NPREs). MATERIAL AND METHODS: Three hundred 
      multiple-choice questions (MCQs) were obtained from the 2024 and 2025 NPRE (150 
      items per year). Each MCQ was entered verbatim into the 3 LLMs using zero-shot 
      prompting in isolated sessions. Responses were scored against the American 
      College of Prosthodontists answer key. Aggregate resident performance by 
      postgraduate year and subject domain was obtained from the ACP database. 
      Descriptive statistics summarized accuracy, and differences among LLMs were 
      evaluated using Cochran Q, chi-squared, or Fisher exact tests (α=.05). RESULTS: 
      Across both years, all LLMs outperformed residents in most content areas, 
      including materials, esthetics, implant prosthodontics, pharmacology, and 
      pathology. Residents performed better than all LLMs only in diagnosis (2024) and 
      in infection control and microbiology (2025). Overall LLM performance for the 
      2024 and 2025 were statistically similar (P=.280 and P=.173, respectively). 
      Gemini 2.5 Pro achieved the highest accuracy in 2024 (78.7%) and 2025 (85.3%). 
      All LLMs scored higher on the 2025 examination compared with 2024, although 
      year-to-year differences were statistically similar (overall, for Gemini 2.5 Pro 
      P=.193, for ChatGPT-5 P=.314, for Claude Sonnet 4 P=.282). CONCLUSIONS: LLMs 
      demonstrated high accuracy on consecutive national prosthodontic examinations and 
      surpassed residents in most domains. Resident advantages in a few areas requiring 
      contextual interpretation indicate domains in which resident accuracy exceeded 
      model accuracy. LLMs show promise as supplemental educational tools, but 
      responsible use requires AI literacy, verification skills, and assessment 
      strategies that emphasize higher-order reasoning.
