TI  - Content analysis of output from generative artificial intelligence chatbots when 
      prompted about breast cancer and alcohol consumption.
PG  - 106278
LID - S0033-3506(26)00147-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.puhe.2026.106278 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVES: Alcohol is a group one carcinogen and contributes to breast cancer 
      risk for women. Awareness of this relationship remains low. Generative artificial 
      intelligence (GenAI) chatbots are an increasingly used source of health 
      information. It is imperative to know what information about alcohol and breast 
      cancer is provided by GenAI chatbots to the public. The objective was to 
      characterize GenAI chatbot output when queried with pre-existing prompts about 
      the relationship between alcohol consumption and breast cancer risk. STUDY 
      DESIGN: Content analysis to characterize GenAI output based on (1) 
      clinical/public health content, and (2) information presentation and formatting. 
      METHODS: We extracted 66 GenAI chatbot outputs about alcohol and breast cancer 
      risk from 22 configurations of 11 GenAI chatbots in June 2025. We used three 
      prompts from the WHO, CDC, and National Breast Cancer Foundation websites 
      regarding alcohol and breast cancer risk. Content analysis was conducted in 
      Dedoose. RESULTS: Similarities and variations in content and presentation and 
      formatting were identified. Nearly all output included information about causal 
      mechanisms (n = 65, 98.48%), and the majority included information about dose 
      dependency (n = 58, 87.88%). About half (n = 35, 53.0%) presented content using a 
      numbered list. Thirty-two (48.48%) did not provide citations/references, while 31 
      (4.97%) included statistics or data about breast cancer and alcohol consumption. 
      Four (6.06%) included phrasing about alcohol's group 1 carcinogen status. 
      CONCLUSIONS: The variability present in GenAI output regarding alcohol 
      consumption and breast cancer risk is a public health concern. Greater 
      standardization of GenAI output and GenAI prompt and output literacy are needed.
