TI  - Beyond literacy to clinical competency: A framework for integrating generative AI 
      into nursing education.
PG  - 107108
LID - S0260-6917(26)00136-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.nedt.2026.107108 [doi]
AB  - PROBLEM: Health systems are rapidly integrating generative artificial 
      intelligence (GenAI) into clinical workflows, introducing safety risks including 
      hallucinations and automation bias. These risks threaten patient safety when 
      frontline nurses lack preparation to critically verify AI-generated outputs. 
      Nurses are uniquely positioned to serve as safety gatekeepers in AI-augmented 
      care; however, realizing this potential requires strengthened GenAI competencies 
      in nursing education. Current curricula have emphasized academic integrity 
      concerns over clinical preparedness, leaving gaps in verification skills, 
      uncertainty communication, and accountability frameworks essential for safe AI 
      integration. BACKGROUND: Established nursing education standards, such as the 
      American Association of Colleges of Nursing (AACN) Essentials, Quality and Safety 
      Education for Nurses (QSEN) competencies, and the Nursing and Midwifery Council 
      (NMC) Future Nurse standards, provide foundational scaffolds for integrating 
      digital and AI-related capabilities into nursing curricula. Yet pedagogical 
      approaches often treat GenAI primarily as a threat to academic integrity rather 
      than as a clinical tool requiring systematic verification, critical appraisal, 
      and accountable use. Integrating GenAI-specific competencies into nursing 
      education is therefore essential for prepare practitioners who can use these 
      technologies while maintaining professional accountability and patient safety. 
      AIM: This discussion paper proposes the Generative AI Nursing Competency (GANC) 
      framework, offering educators and health-system leaders a standards-aligned 
      pathway for preparing nurses to use GenAI without relinquishing professional 
      judgment. The framework specifies seven competency domains: patient-centered 
      assessment, safety-critical clinical reasoning, therapeutic communication, 
      evidence verification, documentation integrity, interprofessional collaboration, 
      and ethical accountability. These domains are translated into observable 
      indicators and assessment exemplars, including simulation and objective 
      structured clinical examination (OSCE) stations. By embedding generative-system 
      risks within routine workflows and emphasizing manual-mode readiness when AI 
      reliability is compromised, GANC provides practical guidance for cultivating 
      nurses as accountable clinical validators in AI-augmented practice environments.
