DP  - 2026 Mar 24
TI  - Ethical AI innovation in healthcare and sustainable development in Bangladesh.
PG  - 1-23
LID - 10.1108/JHOM-12-2025-0862 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE: This study examines how artificial intelligence (AI) innovation and 
      ethical governance influence sustainable healthcare outcomes in Bangladesh, with 
      patient trust and perceived safety acting as mediators and digital and health 
      literacy serving as moderating conditions. DESIGN/METHODOLOGY/APPROACH: A 
      qualitative research design was employed, using semi-structured interviews with 
      five purposively selected stakeholders representing policy, technical, managerial 
      and community health perspectives. Data were analyzed thematically following 
      Braun and Clarke's six-phase approach, using a hybrid deductive-inductive coding 
      strategy supported by NVivo 14. A dual-stage literature review (comprehensive and 
      integrative) informed the conceptual moderated-mediation framework grounded in 
      ethical governance theory, digital transformation theory and the trust-based 
      model of patient engagement. FINDINGS: The findings reveal that AI innovation 
      enhances diagnostic accuracy, efficiency and personalization, while ethical 
      governance -through fairness audits, transparency protocols and accountability 
      measures - builds user confidence and inclusivity. Patient trust and perceived 
      safety emerged as pivotal mechanisms translating technical and ethical design 
      into engagement and adherence. Digital and health literacy significantly 
      moderated these relationships, with low literacy limiting accessibility and 
      equitable impact and targeted literacy interventions enhancing trust and 
      participation. PRACTICAL IMPLICATIONS: The study recommends embedding 
      ethics-by-design principles, culturally adapted interpretability features, and 
      literacy-focused engagement strategies into AI healthcare systems. Policymakers 
      should establish unified ethical AI frameworks, strengthen regulatory enforcement 
      and integrate AI ethics into national health literacy and workforce development 
      initiatives. ORIGINALITY/VALUE: This research offers one of the first empirically 
      grounded moderated-mediation models linking AI innovation, ethical governance, 
      patient trust and literacy to sustainable healthcare outcomes in a low-resource, 
      Muslim-majority context. It advances theory by integrating ethical governance and 
      digital transformation perspectives with behavioral trust models, and provides 
      actionable insights for inclusive and context-sensitive AI adoption in healthcare 
      systems of emerging economies.
