TI  - From Inquiry to Simulation: Leveraging ChatGPT and Chatbots for Dilemma-Based 
      Learning in Healthcare Education.
PG  - 2895-2911
LID - 10.1007/s40670-025-02486-6 [doi]
AB  - The rapid integration of AI in healthcare and medical education requires new 
      pedagogical approaches to enhance AI literacy skills, critical thinking, and 
      decision-making. This study examines a multi-stage framework combining 
      dilemma-based learning with ChatGPT and chatbot development to advance AI 
      literacy in healthcare management students. Through iterative engagement, 
      students progressed from refining prompts in inquiry-based learning to developing 
      chatbots as interactive tools and applying AI-driven simulations for real-world 
      scenario analysis. This study examines the implementation of a structured, 
      multi-stage learning intervention that integrates dilemma-based learning with AI 
      tools, specifically ChatGPT and chatbot development, in undergraduate healthcare 
      management education. A qualitative thematic analysis was conducted on the 
      reflective journals of 71 students, capturing their experiences, skill 
      development, and challenges encountered during AI-enhanced learning. Four main 
      themes emerged: (1) Learning Through Questioning, (2) Learning Through Teaching 
      and Prompt Refinement, (3) Learning Through Simulation, and (4) Struggles and 
      Successes in the Learning Process. Findings highlight that AI-driven learning 
      fosters prompt engineering skills, critical thinking, and engagement in 
      problem-solving while also presenting challenges. Initially, students struggled 
      with formulating effective prompts and structuring chatbot dialogues, but the 
      iterative learning process enabled them to refine analytical skills, engage in 
      multi-perspective dilemmas, and apply AI-based simulations effectively. This 
      study underscores the need to integrate AI literacy and chatbot development into 
      healthcare education to enhance students' ability to navigate complex dilemmas 
      and engage in interactive learning. These insights contribute to the broader 
      discourse on adapting medical education to an AI-driven landscape.
