MH  - SARS-CoV-2
OAB - Digital technologies such as telehealth, mobile health apps, and electronic 
      health records are transforming how family doctors and patients interact. These 
      tools make healthcare more accessible and efficient but also introduce serious 
      risks to data privacy and security. This review examined recent studies on how 
      primary care systems handle these issues and what strategies can help keep 
      patient information safe. We found that many healthcare providers still struggle 
      with protecting sensitive data due to limited resources, outdated technology, and 
      unclear legal rules. Common risks include data breaches, misuse of personal 
      information, and privacy concerns during online consultations. Vulnerable groups, 
      such as older adults or people with low digital literacy, are particularly at 
      risk. Improving data protection in primary care requires more than technical 
      fixes. Stronger privacy laws, staff training, patient-centered design, and 
      systems that consider cultural and ethical aspects are essential. Building 
      digital trust through transparent and secure e-health systems will make 
      healthcare both safer and more equitable.
