TI  - AI literacy, information cocoons, and creativity in healthcare postgraduate 
      education: A latent profile and mediation analysis.
PG  - 107045
LID - S0260-6917(26)00073-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.nedt.2026.107045 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: This study aimed to identify latent subtypes of AI literacy among 
      full-time healthcare postgraduate in China, including those specializing in 
      clinical medicine, nursing, basic medicine, and related medical fields, and to 
      examine the mediating role of information cocoons in the relationship between AI 
      literacy types and creativity. METHODS: From April to August 2025, 278 full-time 
      healthcare postgraduates in China were surveyed using scales for AI literacy, 
      information cocoons, and creativity. Data were analyzed using Mplus 8.3 for 
      Latent Profile Analysis and Mediation Analysis, and SPSS 27.0 for univariate and 
      multivariate analyses. RESULTS: (1) Four AI literacy subtypes were identified: 
      Lowest Level-Least Skilled (5.40%), Lower Medium Level-Least Ethical (24.46%), 
      Upper Medium Level-Weaker Attitude (37.41%), and Highest Level-Balanced (32.73%). 
      (2) Logistic regression analysis indicated that gender and grade were significant 
      predictors of AI literacy classification (X(2) = 1.48, p < 0.001; X(2) = 76.75, 
      p < 0.001). (3) Person-centered analysis (with the Lowest Level-Least Skilled 
      group as the reference group) revealed that the Upper Medium Level-Weaker 
      Attitude (β = -0.513, p < 0.001) and Highest Level-Balanced (β = -0.470, 
      p < 0.001) groups had significant negative effects on information cocoons, while 
      the Lower Medium Level-Least Ethical group showed a positive effect (β = 0.121, 
      p = 0.035). Information cocoons maintained a significant negative effect on 
      creativity (β = -0.380, p < 0.001). Further effect decomposition demonstrated 
      significant mediating effects of information cocoons in the relationships between 
      the Lower Medium Level-Least Ethical, Upper Medium Level-Weaker Attitude, and 
      Highest Level-Balanced groups and creativity, with bootstrap confidence intervals 
      of (-0.251, -0.009), (0.277, 0.671), and (0.248, 0.669), accounting for -17.35%, 
      36.73%, and 20.13%of the total effect, respectively. CONCLUSION: This study 
      reveals heterogeneity in AI literacy among Chinese full-time healthcare 
      postgraduates and demonstrates that information cocoons partially mediate the 
      relationship between all AI literacy subtypes and creativity. These findings 
      provide important insights for enhancing graduate students' creativity in the 
      context of Generative AI.
