TI  - A Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, and Threats Analysis of Generative 
      Artificial Intelligence in Aphasia Management With Proposed Ethical Guidelines.
PG  - 402-421
LID - 10.1044/2025_AJSLP-25-00262 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE: Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is rapidly emerging as a 
      transformative technology in aphasia management, necessitating judicious and 
      structured evaluation. The purpose of this clinical focus article is to apply a 
      Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, and Threats (SWOT) analysis framework to 
      systematically evaluate the potential role and implications of GenAI tools in 
      aphasia management and to offer preliminary ethical considerations and 
      recommendations for their responsible use in clinical contexts. METHOD: This 
      clinical focus article offers initial SWOT analysis of GenAI tools by members of 
      a GenAI in aphasia management working group. The clinical domains within the 
      analysis include screening and assessment, intervention, augmentative and 
      alternative communication and digital accessibility, counseling and care partner 
      education, and clinical documentation. RESULTS: The SWOT analysis identified 
      GenAI's potential for enhancing screening and assessment, personalizing 
      treatment, supporting care partners, and streamlining clinical documentation. The 
      analysis also highlighted issues related to problematic outputs; digital literacy 
      requirements; vulnerability to deepfakes, phishing, and medical misinformation; 
      economic and accessibility constraints; and concerns over autonomy and privacy. 
      Preliminary ethical analysis was subsequently performed to establish a basis for 
      ethical uses of GenAI in aphasia management, despite the dynamic nature of the 
      GenAI landscape. CONCLUSIONS: GenAI presents valuable opportunities to advance 
      aphasia management, but substantial weaknesses and threats underscore the need 
      for cautious integration into clinical practice. Proactive collaboration among 
      clinicians, people with aphasia, institutions, and policymakers is necessary to 
      help maximize clinical benefits while preserving essential human elements in 
      aphasia care and addressing concerns related to patient privacy and 
      accessibility. SUPPLEMENTAL MATERIAL: https://doi.org/10.23641/asha.31244260.
