TI  - Health Communication at a Crossroads as the Journal of Health Communication 
      Begins Its 4(th) Decade of Publication.
PG  - 101-103
LID - 10.1080/10810730.2026.2629287 [doi]
AB  - As the Journal of Health Communication enters its fourth decade in 2026, the 
      field confronts a period of profound transformation. AI, platform-mediated 
      communication, and the fragmenting of information environments reshape health 
      communication and information amid persistent global inequities and declining 
      governing confidence. This editorial reflects on the Journal's legacy while 
      articulating a vision that centers the quality, governance, and social 
      responsibilities of the discipline. We argue for a shift beyond individual-level 
      models toward greater attention to institutional accountability, health literacy 
      as a systemic model, and the communicative consequences of digital and AI-driven 
      systems. Reaffirming a commitment to methodological rigor and global 
      perspectives, we invite scholarship that advances theory and practice in response 
      to the evolving conditions that now define health communication.
