TI  - Drawing in craniofacial surgery: cognitive, educational, and communicative roles.
PG  - 17-22
LID - 10.1080/17453054.2026.2625749 [doi]
AB  - Drawing has long accompanied surgical training and practice. In craniofacial 
      surgery - where three-dimensional deformities and long treatment pathways are 
      common - drawing may support observation, operative planning, documentation, 
      education, communication, and psychosocial care. This article reviews historical 
      perspectives and contemporary literature, highlighting drawing - including 
      emerging digital approaches - as a complementary, pragmatic, and patient-centred 
      tool in modern craniofacial practice. It also considers emerging digital and 
      AI-supported tools and proposes directions for future research on learning, 
      communication, and patient experience.
