PL  - England
TA  - Res Involv Engagem
JT  - Research involvement and engagement
JID - 101708164
PMC - PMC12574169
OAB - This paper describes a new framework to guide how people and patients can be 
      involved in shaping a research grant application. In this example, we show how 
      people with lived experience of chronic illness were involved in all stages of 
      shaping a research grant. The grant aimed to explore the use of artificial 
      intelligence (AI) to help create personalised exercise plans in general 
      practice.We asked for feedback and ideas from people with chronic illness, and 
      their carers, at four key stages of the grant-writing process. We ran a 
      face-to-face workshop with seven people, from which four participants agreed to 
      join a lived experience advisory panel (LEAP). This panel continued to be 
      involved as the project progressed, reviewing drafts of the grant application as 
      it was written. Their input helped to shape research questions and design, 
      improve the details of the proposal, and ensure the research was relevant and 
      inclusive to what the public wants. This paper provides a new method for 
      researchers to plan and carry out meaningful patient and public involvement to 
      improve grant applications, and ensure future research projects are relevant for 
      the people who will use them, leading to more practical and beneficial outcomes 
      for patients and the public.
