TI  - How to breakthrough disparities in scientific literacy of different family 
      background: The mediating role of self-directed learning efficacy and the 
      moderating role of family support.
PG  - 105817
LID - S0001-6918(25)01130-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.actpsy.2025.105817 [doi]
AB  - In the 21st century, dominated by artificial intelligence and technological 
      innovation, scientific literacy has become a core competency for global citizens. 
      As a crucial environment for student development, the family not only provides 
      ascribed capital (e.g., economic capital, cultural capital) but also offers 
      emotional support, influencing students' self-directed learning efficacy, thereby 
      playing a pivotal role in cultivating scientific literacy that transcends 
      traditional perceptions. Grounded in social cognitive theory, this study utilizes 
      PISA 2022 data from six East Asian regions (China's Hong Kong SAR, China's Macao 
      SAR, China's Taiwan Province, Singapore, South Korea, Japan), involving 34,968 
      students to investigate how family background influences scientific literacy. The 
      results indicate: (1) Family background significantly and positively predicts 
      students' scientific literacy. (2) Students' self-directed learning efficacy 
      partially mediates the relationship between family background and scientific 
      literacy. (3) Family support moderates the first half of this mediating pathway. 
      Based on these findings, we recommend: prioritizing support for disadvantaged 
      family backgrounds to promote equity in scientific literacy; enhancing 
      self-directed learning efficacy to foster higher-order scientific thinking; and 
      emphasizing family emotional support to cultivate a nurturing home environment.
