OAB - ”Dr. Google,” is used by individuals to seek health-related information for 
      common issues like tendon pain. However, it is unknown if the information found 
      online is trustworthy and easy to understand. This study investigated what people 
      in Italy search for online about the most common tendinopathies: Achilles, knee, 
      shoulder, and elbow. The most popular web pages were examined for credibility 
      (trustworthiness), readability (easy to understand), and how web page content 
      aligns with best available evidence. Patients with tendon pain were consulted in 
      this research project.Our findings demonstrated that online information is often 
      poor quality. Most web pages used outdated terms like “tendinitis” which may give 
      end-users incorrect information about their condition. It was found that very few 
      web pages were trustworthy and unbiased. The most visited web pages were found to 
      have poor readability, even for people with a high level of education. 
      Furthermore, advice for tendinopathy conditions viewed online rarely aligned with 
      expert recommendations.Poor-quality online information can affect the end-users 
      understanding of their health and the subsequent choices they make. There is a 
      need for healthcare professionals to help create clear and accurate online 
      resources for tendinopathy, using terminology that facilitates appropriate 
      assessment and management of the condition.
OABL- eng
OTO - NOTNLM
OT  - E-health
OT  - health literacy
OT  - tendinopathy
OT  - tendon
OT  - web data
COIS- Why this matters: Millions of Italians turn to “Dr Google” for tendon pain 
      advice, yet the caliber of that information had never been systematically 
      assessed; poor-quality online content can distort expectations, influence 
      treatment choices and ultimately affect outcomes. Aims: (1) Map the most-searched 
      keywords and questions about Achilles, knee, rotator-cuff and elbow tendons; (2) 
      appraise the credibility (QUEST), readability (Gulpease) and evidence-alignment 
      of the top-clicked web pages; (3) involve patients to ensure real-world 
      relevance. Patient partnership: Four individuals with lived tendinopathy 
      experience co-selected search terms and interpreted findings, embedding a 
      patient-centered lens throughout the study. Design and data sources: 
      Cross-sectional analysis of Italian Google™ searches captured via Semrush (01 Dec 
      2024). The 88 highest-traffic pages answering the top questions were scored with 
      QUEST (0–28) and Gulpease (0–100), then deductively coded against current 
      clinical-practice guidelines. Terminology gap: “Tendinitis” dominated both 
      searches and web pages, whereas the guideline-endorsed term “tendinopathy” was 
      absent, indicating a persistent disconnect between scientific consensus and 
      public discourse. Credibility crisis: Mean QUEST scores were low; only 2 pages 
      (2.2%) met the threshold for unbiased information, and fewer than 8% cited 
      primary research. Commercial conflicts of interest were common. Readability 
      roadblock: 100% of pages were “almost incomprehensible” for readers with 
      elementary education, and 61% remained “difficult” even for those with higher 
      education, underscoring a substantial e-health-literacy barrier. Evidence 
      mismatch: Across all tendons, fewer than half of pages reflected 
      guideline-recommended diagnosis or treatment advice, and none provided 
      evidence-based prognostic timelines, leaving patients prone to unrealistic 
      recovery expectations. Behavioral insight: The most frequent queries centered on 
      “how to treat” or “how to reduce inflammation,” suggesting a public perception of 
      tendinopathy as an acute, inflammatory issue amendable to quick fixes, which may 
      foster nocebo beliefs and poorer adherence to exercise-based care. Clinical and 
      policy implications: Clinicians should (i) routinely explore patients’ online 
      information sources, (ii) correct misleading “-itis” terminology, and (iii) 
      direct them to readable, evidence-based resources. Professional bodies and 
      search-engine stakeholders must collaborate to raise the visibility of 
      high-quality Italian-language content, while researchers should test whether such 
      interventions translate into better pain and function outcomes. The authors have 
      no relevant affiliations or financial involvement with any organization or entity 
      with a financial interest in or financial conflict with the subject matter or 
      materials discussed in the manuscript. This includes employment, consultancies, 
      honoraria, stock ownership or options, expert testimony, grants or patents 
      received or pending, or royalties. No writing assistance was utilized in the 
      production of this manuscript. Giacomo Rossettini is a member of the Pain 
      Management Editorial Board. They were not involved in any editorial decisions 
      related to the publication of this article, and author details were not made 
      available to the article’s peer reviewers as per the journal’s double-anonymized 
      peer review policy.
