OAB - Disinformation and Foreign Information Manipulation and Interference (FIMI) 
      currently poses a growing threat to our democratic societies. Among the entities 
      addressing its mitigation, fact-checking organizations have emerged and 
      consolidated as pivotal contributors, playing a central role in the 
      implementation of reactive strategies. However, these approaches face significant 
      limitations that can affect their effectiveness. In recent years, the European 
      Union has adopted a determined approach to strengthening citizens' media and 
      digital literacy, positioning it as a fundamental preventive tool within its 
      broader strategies. In this context, fact-checking organizations, leveraging 
      their role of public dominance, are expanding their scope of action, developing 
      media literacy initiatives as a complementary activity to their traditional 
      fact-checking work. The research examines, based on a sample of fact-checking 
      organizations with membership in the International Fact-Checking Network (IFCN), 
      the types of literacy initiatives carried out at two distinct time points. The 
      analysis concludes a moderate yet growing presence of these activities, primarily 
      in the form of online courses and materials. A connection is also observed with 
      externally funded programs and projects, mainly those financed by the EU. 
      Differences are noted in the target audiences of these activities, which can be 
      linked to local sociodemographic patterns. The study highlights the interest in 
      promoting the role of these organizations, considering their ability to reach 
      audiences outside formal education systems who are particularly vulnerable to 
      disinformation and its effects.
