PMID- 40494217
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20250802
LR  - 20250807
IS  - 1872-8243 (Electronic)
IS  - 1386-5056 (Linking)
VI  - 203
DP  - 2025 Nov
TI  - Patient perspectives on artificial intelligence in healthcare: A global scoping 
      review of benefits, ethical concerns, and implementation strategies.
PG  - 106007
LID - S1386-5056(25)00224-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2025.106007 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVES: The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into healthcare 
      offers opportunities for improving patient care, yet successful implementation 
      relies on patient acceptance. Understanding how patients perceive AI technologies 
      in healthcare is critical for developing strategies that address their concerns 
      while leveraging perceived benefits. This scoping review, the first comprehensive 
      synthesis of patient concerns across diverse healthcare contexts, aims to 
      systematically identify and synthesize evidence regarding patients' perceptions 
      of AI in healthcare, including perceived benefits, concerns, and factors 
      influencing acceptance. METHODS: Following PRISMA-ScR (PRISMA for scoping 
      reviews) guidelines, a comprehensive search was conducted across Scopus, PubMed, 
      and Web of Science for empirical studies published between January 2014 and June 
      2024. The review identified 38 studies meeting eligibility criteria. Data 
      extraction focused on study characteristics, AI applications, healthcare domains, 
      and patient-reported perceptions organized into thematic categories. RESULTS: The 
      scoping review identified 38 studies across 18 countries, with over 75% of 
      patients expressing notable benefits of AI in healthcare, including improved 
      diagnostic accuracy (particularly in medical imaging), enhanced efficiency, time 
      savings, and personalized care. However, approximately 50-70% of patients had 
      substantial concerns about the loss of human connection in healthcare delivery, 
      reliability, and safety uncertainties, privacy and data security risks, and 
      issues with trust, transparency, and patient autonomy. Acceptance factors 
      clustered into demographic factors (age, education, digital literacy), health 
      condition specifics, clinical involvement models, and organizational/systemic 
      factors. An integrated framework illustrating the multifaceted nature of patient 
      acceptance of AI in healthcare is proposed. CONCLUSIONS: Patient acceptance of AI 
      encompasses a complex interplay of individual characteristics, technology design 
      features, healthcare contextual factors and systemic considerations. The insights 
      of this scoping review call for human-centric AI design and regulatory frameworks 
      prioritizing patient autonomy. Successful implementation strategies should 
      maintain human oversight, ensure transparent communication about AI capabilities 
      and limitations, tailor approaches to specific contexts, and engage patients as 
      stakeholders in development and implementation. Future research should explore 
      diverse cultural contexts and develop interventions to enhance patient education 
      and engagement with healthcare AI.
