TI  - Pharmacy students' perceptions of artificial intelligence integration in pharmacy 
      practice: Ethical challenges in multiple countries of the MENA region.
PG  - 102397
LID - S1877-1297(25)00118-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.cptl.2025.102397 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into pharmacy 
      practice has the potential to advance learning experiences and prepare future 
      pharmacists for evolving healthcare needs. However, it also raises ethical 
      considerations that need to be addressed carefully. This study aimed to explore 
      pharmacy students' attitudes regarding AI integration into their future pharmacy 
      practice. METHODS: A cross-sectional design was employed, utilizing a validated 
      online questionnaire administered to pharmacy students from diverse demographic 
      backgrounds in multiple countries of the Middle East and North Africa (MENA) 
      region from August 2022 to January 2023. Demographic, education, and work 
      information data were, respectively, collected from study participants. In 
      addition, technology literacy and AI familiarity were collected using a Likert 
      scale on skill and a Likert scale on familiarity. Finally, participants' concerns 
      and perceived barriers regarding AI integration were collected based on a Likert 
      scale on agreement. RESULTS: A total of 702 pharmacy students participated in the 
      study, with the majority being female (72.8 %), enrolled in public universities 
      (55.6 %), and not employed (64.2 %). Participants expressed a generally negative 
      attitude towards AI integration, where 56.2-70.8 % of respondents agreed/strongly 
      agreed to concerns/barriers including patient data privacy (62.0 %), 
      susceptibility to hacking (56.2 %), potential job displacement (69.3 %), cost 
      limitations (66.8 %), access (69.1 %), the absence of regulations (68.1 %), and 
      training (70.4 %), physicians' reluctance (65.1 %), and patient apprehension 
      (70.8 %). Factors including country of residence, academic year, cumulative GPA, 
      work status, technology literacy, and AI understanding influenced participants' 
      attitudes. Positive correlations were found between attitude score and 
      tech-savviness (r = 0.174), and AI understanding (r = 0.155). CONCLUSION: 
      Pharmacy students from multiple countries in the MENA region express significant 
      ethical and practical concerns about AI's integration into their future practice. 
      These findings underscore the need for incorporating AI education within pharmacy 
      curricula, alongside the development of robust ethical guidelines and regulatory 
      policies. Addressing students' concerns is crucial to ensuring ethical, 
      equitable, and beneficial AI integration in future pharmacy practice.
