TI  - Are Two Heads Better Than One?: The Effect of Student-AI Collaboration on 
      Students' Learning Task Performance.
PG  - 365-375
LID - 10.1007/s11528-022-00788-9 [doi]
AB  - A growing number of educators expect that artificial intelligence (AI) will 
      augment students' capacities and rapidly transform the teaching and learning 
      practice. However, there is a lack of convincing evidence on the effects of 
      Student-AI Collaboration (SAC) on a learning task's performance. A critical 
      examination of the effects on students' learning performance is a crucial step in 
      understanding the potential benefits of SAC on learning. Through a repeated 
      measure experiment participated by 20 undergraduate students in South Korea, this 
      study examined the effects of SAC on a public advertisement drawing task. The 
      findings revealed that SAC significantly affects creativity in content, 
      expressivity in expression, and public utility in effectiveness varied depending 
      on students' attitude toward AI or on the level of drawing skill. Implications 
      for the design of educational AI and AI literacy education are discussed.
