TI  - Using support vector machines to identify literacy skills: Evidence from eye 
      movements.
PG  - 887-895
LID - 10.3758/s13428-016-0748-7 [doi]
AB  - Is inferring readers' literacy skills possible by analyzing their eye movements 
      during text reading? This study used Support Vector Machines (SVM) to analyze eye 
      movement data from 61 undergraduate students who read a multiple-paragraph, 
      multiple-topic expository text. Forward fixation time, first-pass rereading time, 
      second-pass fixation time, and regression path reading time on different regions 
      of the text were provided as features. The SVM classification algorithm assisted 
      in distinguishing high-literacy-skilled readers from low-literacy-skilled readers 
      with 80.3 % accuracy. Results demonstrate the effectiveness of combining eye 
      tracking and machine learning techniques to detect readers with low literacy 
      skills, and suggest that such approaches can be potentially used in predicting 
      other cognitive abilities.
